    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d453">

                <pb facs="0587" n="473" sameAs="frus:frus1924v01/pg473"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.93/79: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kellogg</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-08-01T15:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 1,
                                1924—3 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received August 1—2:01
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>302. I have had a conversation at the Foreign Office based on Department’s
                    telegram 236 of July 28 [<hi rendition="#italic">26</hi>]<anchor n="98"
                        xml:id="d453-f1"/> with the result that I am informed that the Foreign
                    Office is telegraphing the British Ambassador in Japan to suggest to the
                    Japanese Government that it would be well for the great powers to inform the
                    Chinese Government that while there is disorder and disunion in China and while
                    adequate protection is not given to the rights of foreign <pb facs="0588"
                        n="474" xml:id="pg474"/> nationals they are not willing to change the rank
                    of their missions in China. The Foreign Office is authorizing the British
                    Ambassador in Japan to say that instructions will be sent to the British
                    Minister in China to act with the Japanese Minister there if the Japanese will
                    instruct their Minister in this sense. It is the hope of the Foreign Office that
                    similar action at Tokyo will be taken by the Department of State.</p>
                <p>Copy of this telegram has been sent to European Information Center.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
